<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements of the three executables that run as the app: Contents/MacOS/open-live-ingest-app
  (the desktop app, the bundle's main executable), Contents/MacOS/open-live-ingest (the CLI,
  the process TCC sees over SSH) and Contents/MacOS/strom. build-macos-app.sh signs them with
  these and the hardened runtime; gst-plugin-scanner (which only loads plugins to list them)
  gets none. The app's webview needs nothing more: WKWebView runs its JIT in Apple's
  WebContent process, not in the app.

  com.apple.security.device.camera       Strom opens the cameras (GStreamer's
                                          avfvideosrc). Under the hardened runtime a
                                          process without it cannot use a camera at all.
  com.apple.security.device.audio-input  The same for microphones and audio interfaces
                                          (osxaudiosrc).

  This file grants no hardened-runtime exception, so a Developer ID build keeps library
  validation on: every library and plugin Strom and the scanner load lives in the bundle
  and is signed with the same Team ID as the executables. An ad hoc build has no Team ID,
  and macOS 27 then refuses to map the bundled libraries into strom ("different Team
  IDs", #66). For an ad hoc build only, build-macos-app.sh therefore signs strom,
  open-live-ingest, open-live-ingest-app and gst-plugin-scanner with this file plus
  com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation (the scanner with that key alone),
  and its bundle check refuses that key on any other signature.

  One hardened-runtime exception is granted, to strom alone, and not in this file:
  build-macos-app.sh signs Contents/MacOS/strom with a copy of this file plus

  com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit         GStreamer's ORC compiles the inner loops of
                                          videoconvert, videoscale, audioconvert and the
                                          like to machine code at run time. The Developer
                                          ID build of v0.5.0 showed that without the key
                                          the hardened runtime refuses ORC's write and
                                          exec mapping ("ORC: ERROR: ... Failed to create
                                          write and exec mmap regions", #110, #113), and
                                          ORC falls back to slower C code, more CPU load
                                          on the uplink's pipeline.

  It is kept out of this file so the app, the CLI and gst-plugin-scanner, which run no
  pipeline, never get it; the bundle check requires it on strom and refuses it on every
  other signature. Library validation stays on for a Developer ID build: allow-jit only
  lets strom map memory for code it generates itself (MAP_JIT), it does not let strom
  load a library signed by another team. Add another exception only when a Developer ID
  build shows it is needed, with the reason.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.device.camera</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
